  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Through FY2026 to date federal contract obligations to Tennessee total $9.9B across 16,792 awards, with an average award size of $588K. Obligations are down 9.9% year over year. The Department of Energy accounts for the largest share of buying at $6.4B, well ahead of the Department of Defense at $1.4B and the Department of Justice at $707M. Two vendors dominate receipts: CONSOLIDATED NUCLEAR SECURITY, LLC with $2.9B and UT-BATTELLE LLC with $2.4B. Facilities Support Services is the biggest industry by obligations at $5.4B, followed by Engineering Services at $684M and Remediation Services at $612M. Fiscal-year obligations by year show $10.8B in FY21, $11.4B in FY22, $11.2B in FY23, $10.8B in FY24, $11B in FY25, and $9.9B in FY26 to date. These figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial because fiscal year to date reporting is incomplete.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is federal buying in Tennessee?",
        "body": "Buying is highly concentrated. The Department of Energy obligates $6.4B, the single largest buyer, followed by the Department of Defense at $1.4B and the Department of Justice at $707M. Facilities Support Services accounts for $5.4B of obligations, making that industry the largest by value."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who captures the biggest shares of Tennessee contract dollars?",
        "body": "A small set of vendors captures a large share of obligations. CONSOLIDATED NUCLEAR SECURITY, LLC received $2.9B and UT-BATTELLE LLC received $2.4B. Other top vendors include METROPOLITAN SECURITY SERVICES, INC. at $517M and UNITED CLEANUP OAK RIDGE LLC at $454M."
      }
    ]
  },
